## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over subsequences with a fixed length and pairwise costs, plus explicit reconstruction. It moves beyond contiguous partitions by requiring last-step linkage through prior picks.

## Key Concepts
- Subsequence DP with fixed length
- Min-over-predecessor recurrence
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and practical optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function solve(A, m){
  const n=A.length;
  const dp=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(m+1).fill(Infinity));
  const par=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(m+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][1]=0;
  for(let t=2;t<=m;t++){
    for(let i=t-1;i<n;i++){
      for(let j=t-2;j<i;j++){
        const c=dp[j][t-1]+Math.abs(A[i]-A[j]);
        if(c<dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=c; par[i][t]=j; }
      }
    }
  }
  let best=Infinity,bestI=-1;
  for(let i=m-1;i<n;i++){ if(dp[i][m]<best){ best=dp[i][m]; bestI=i; } }
  const path=[];
  let cur=bestI, t=m;
  while(t>=1){
    path.push(cur);
    cur=par[cur][t];
    t--;
  }
  path.reverse();
  return {cost:best, indices:path};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This problem evaluates dynamic programming expertise across string partitioning with fixed segment counts, numeric parsing constraints, and solution reconstruction—fundamental skills for real-world interview scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic programming over string prefixes and partition counts
- Handling substring numeric values with leading-zero validation
- Solution path reconstruction for determining optimal cut positions
- Time and space complexity optimization techniques

## Code Example

```javascript
function minSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;

  // Helper to get numeric value of substring [l, r)
  const getValue = (l, r) => {
    if (s[l] === '0' && r - l > 1) return null; // leading zero
    return Number(s.slice(l, r));
  };

  const dp = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const parent = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      for (let j = t - 1; j < i; j++) {
        const val = getValue(j, i);
        if (val !== null && dp[j][t-1] + val < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[j][t-1] + val;
          parent[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct cuts
  const cuts = [];
  let curr = n, currT = k;
  while (currT > 0) {
    const prev = parent[curr][currT];
    cuts.push(prev);
    curr = prev;
    currT--;
  }
  cuts.pop(); // remove 0
  cuts.reverse();

  return { minSum: dp[n][k], cuts };
}
```

## Notes

- The DP explores all possible cut positions while respecting the k-substring constraint
- Leading zero validation is crucial for correctness
- The parent array enables O(k) reconstruction of optimal cuts

